{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/357320-sorcerer-20th-anniversary-edition","id":357320,"title":"Sorcerer 20th Anniversary Edition","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-01-02T00:49:46-08:00","id":357320,"title":"Sorcerer 20th Anniversary Edition","content":"Those of you who have seen my posts on the <b>Mage: the Ascension</b> forum probably know that I\u2019ve got some rather strong opinions about <b>Sorcerer</b>; if you don\u2019t, check out the Acolyte Magic post linked to in the Ascension hacks found in my sig. The thing is, though, that that hack only addresses half of the issue of Sleeper magicians as I see it: it takes the concept of non-Awakened proto-mages who can work a limited form of Magick because their Avatars are struggling to Awaken and replaces the Hedge Magic rules basis with a minor variation of <b>Mage</b>\u2019s Sphere Magick. What it <i>doesn\u2019t</i> do is to take the Hedge Magic rules and refine them into a Sorcery system that stands on its own. <br /> <br /> To be fair, the original World of Darkness only had one book in it that addressed Hedge Magicians as their own thing: the 1997 book <b>World of Darkness: Sorcerer</b>, <i>The Hedge Wizard\u2019s Handbook</i>. Prior to that, the most comprehensive take on Hedge Magic was in the Mage supplement <b>Ascension\u2019s Right Hand</b>; and after that, Hedge Magic was put back under the Mage line with <b>Sorcerer Revised</b>. <br /> <br /> Let me also note that <b>Mage 20th Anniversary Edition</b> is technically two years late now, and <b>Wraith 20th Anniversary Edition</b> has also missed the year in which its title would technically be accurate. Not judging; just saying \u2014 I definitely agree with the \u201cbetter late and done right than on time and done wrong\u201d philosophy at work here. But it <i>does</i> mean that Wraith 20 will probably be coming out some time in 2015\u20132016, and Changeling 20 will probably be released 2016\u20132017. And hey, 2017 will be the 20th anniversary of <b>Sorcerer</b>\u2026<br /> <br /> There are actually two things I\u2019d like to discuss here: first, the viability of a 20th Anniversary Edition for <b>Sorcerer</b>; and second, what you\u2019d want to see in such a book if it were actually to be published. <br /> <br /> On the first point, it\u2019s worth noting that White Wolf didn\u2019t stop publishing games in 2015: a hypothetical <b>Sorcerer 20</b> would be competing with <b>Kindred of the East 20</b> and <b>Mummy 20</b> at the very least. Personally, I\u2019d be OK with <b>Kindred of the East</b> never getting a commemorative edition at all; or if it gets a new edition, having it published closer to its 25th anniversary. I definitely would be more interested in <b>Sorcerer</b> than in <b>Kindred of the East</b>. As for <b>Mummy</b>, that\u2019s a tougher call for me; but when push comes to shove, I\u2019d be more interested in a <b>Mummy: the Resurrection</b> 20th Anniversary Edition. <br /> <br /> In terms of what I\u2019d like to see in a <b>Sorcerer 20</b>: in a nutshell, an overhaul of the Hedge Magic system from the perspective of sorcerers being their own thing rather than a Mage spin-off, and with a consistent design philosophy rather than the ad hoc hodge-podge accumulation of Paths that were featured in the first two editions. What this means:<br /> <br /> \u2022 The notion that Hedge Magic has anything to do with Sleeper Avatars or that it\u2019s empowered by the magician\u2019s beliefs should be relegated to a small entry in a crossover section, where it would be presented as an unproven theory put forth by a mage about what sorcery is. This theory would get equal time with counterparts put forth by the likes of werewolves (\u201csorcery is a manifestation of the Wyld\u201d, or some other theory about how sorcery traces back to the Triat, Gaia, or some other element of <b>Werewolf</b>\u2019s metaphysics) or changelings (who would probably explain sorcery as something rooted in Glamour); and all such theories would be presented in the spirit of the parable of the Blind Men and the Elephant. Sorcerers would have their own explanation for what sorcery is, which would be based heavily on the one provided in the 1997 Sorcerer book: the world of darkness is itself inherently magical and full of arcane Mysteries, and sorcerers are individuals who have the curiosity and discipline neccessary to harness the world\u2019s secrets for their own ends. <br /> <br /> \u2022 Mechanically, I\u2019d start with the Paths covered in <b>Sorcerer Revised</b> plus the ones first published in the <b>Dreamspeakers Tradition Book, Revised</b>; but I\u2019d take a back-to-basics approach to the write-ups, presenting them in the same way as the Paths featured in <b>Hunters Hunted II</b>. In fact, I\u2019d do it so that the Paths featured in HH2 could be pasted into S20 almost verbatim. This might involve downgrading some Paths such as Hellfire or boosting others so that the Paths presented have a more even spread of utility. It might also involve recasting some existing Paths as minor variants of other existing Paths \u2014 or at least clarifying what their relative strengths and weaknesses are. <br /> <br /> \u2022 The book would <i>not</i> feature Psychic Phenomena; I\u2019d save that for a companion book devoted exclusively to psychics, mediums, and their ilk. This would primarily be to free up space: I\u2019d expect <b>Sorcerer 20</b>\u2019s word count to increase substantially over what <b>Sorcerer Revised</b> got, even <i>without</i> Psychic Phenomena. <br /> <br /> \u2022 Things like Aspects or mana would be relegated to optional systems that the Storyteller could introduce if he wants the sorcerers in his chronicle to be more effective. Likewise, the \u201cdoesn\u2019t work in front of witnesses\u201d rule would be turned into an optional Flaw instead of a universal restriction on Hedge Magic. <br /> <br /> \u2022 Per the 1997 edition of <b>Sorcerer</b>, each magician would have a magical style that determines what magical instruments and practices he uses to work his magic. This would be cribbed directly from <b>M20</b>; but in keeping with the aforementioned HH2 compatibility issue, said magical tools would be presented as an optional enhancement to the basic Path write-up, providing some sort of benefit (such as an activation cost discount) if you use the tools, and/or letting you use a different Ability in the Path\u2019s dice pool. Such rules would <i>not</i> be presented as a necessary part of the Sorcery rules, however. <br /> <br /> \u2022 In keeping with the aforementioned \u201cblind men and the elephant\u201d analogy, I\u2019d want to introduce rules options that tweak the Sorcery rules in ways that bring them more in line with the underlying metaphysics of games like Werewolf, Wraith, or Changeling. Werewolf-based options, for instance, would tend toward a more animistic approach to magic \u2014 say, by having most or all Paths operate off of Social Attributes rather than Mental ones, or somehow tying into Gnosis. A Changeling-based option might present Sorcery as something that\u2019s opposed by Banality and/or concealed by the Mists, or might introduce some sort of Realms-like enhancement similar to how the Mage-based options include an Aspects-like enhancement. And so on. <br /> <br /> Thoughts?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":421,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","user":"Dataweaver","date":". 09-19-2018, 12:46 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-02T02:38:03-08:00","id":357357,"title":"","content":"I don't think Sorcerer could fill a slot as a 20th core book. So any issue with it competing with other books is lost on me. 20th supplements have been released both via KS and under the normal supplement design process that Onyx Path uses. The DAV20 and Wr20 didn't stop W20 Umbra from coming out. Sorcerer 20 is in the same boat as the Umbra, not a major core book; in terms of viability.<br /> <br /> And if we're getting Sorecer 20th edition book, I don't want WOD: Sorcerer with a newer shine. The 20th books are best when they include all of the source material and gather it together for us combined with some tweaking some of the more broken rules; and not just the rules.<br /> <br /> That means is should be a M20 supplement. That means Awakened mage organizations should get full coverage (and for M20 that means everyone, not just the Traditions) as well as Sorcerer specific organizations (something Sorcerer Revised included in as much if not more detail than they did when discussing the Traditions and Conventions; even if less detail than WOD: Sorcerer). 2/3 books that cover Sorcerers are Mage books, even if you don't like it. The majority of sorcerers are either affiliated with Mage based organizations, or their own, and M20 is already stated as having a stronger support for Crafts. This makes supporting sorcerer specific organizations the same as M20 supporting more Crafts.<br /> <br /> This also means Psychics should be in. Rites of the Blood for V20 got ~40 more pages than WOD: Sorcerer, or Sorcerer Revised. That's more then enough to include expanded details on Sorcerer organizations (both with existing Mage groups and their own unique ones) without needing to cut Psychics for a second book. The Faces of Sorcery chapter of WOD:S is only 30 pages. The TB: Dreamspealer Rev. Static Shaman entry is under 2 pages. Sorcerer Rev. had just over 10 pages already allotted to Sorcerer unique organizations. So you've talked about adding ~21 pages of material to Sorcerer Revised's length. Using up just half of the extra space it would get by being expanded to the same size as Rites of the Blood. There's no reason to be stingy here.<br /> <br /> There's also no need to have explanations for Numina for all the game lines. The idea that hedge mages might be using their powers as focused through their Avatars is not really presented as the absolute truth of how Numina exist (and there's a reason why there's a Merit for proto-mages rather than it being the base-line assumption). Werewolves and changelings can explain Numina however they want, just like mages can. We don't need lots of attention on crossover stuff because most characters in most of the game lines aren't mystic theory junkies like mages are. Werewolves don't know about true vs. hedge magic, they can't really tell the difference and explaining it to them is most academic towards how the Garou are presented as feeling about human magics. The same goes for the fae. The existing books have book far less word count into this topic than you have in these threads.<br /> <br /> Trying to have a whole set of Sorcery rules tweaks for different game line metaphysics is also just flat out unnecessary. There don't need to be Gnosis based Numina, or Glamour powered Psychics. How are these any better than, &quot;Sorcerers are proto-mages&quot; instead of them being their own thing?<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-02T10:00:35-08:00","id":357564,"title":"","content":"From a completely external perspective, I don't think it would fly as an independent product. There isn't a core rabid Sorcerer fan base like there is for Wraith or Changeling. There doesn't even appear to be one to the degree of HtR or Demon. From the tenor of comments I've seen, it's about as popular as, say, Mummy. I think we'll see people lining up to buy Kindred of the East 20+ before we see people putting money down for a Sorcerer20 core book kicking off a game line Kickstarter or the like.<br /> <br /> Second, What does Sorcerer add to the WoD overall? As a major line it falls into something along the lines of re-treading the same thematic ground that Mage does. Again, looking in from outside, Vampire is about bloodsucking nighwalkers, Werewolf is about people who get big, hairy and dangerous (and also are the bloody claw of the mutant lovechild of Greenpeace and animism), Wraith is what immaterial dead people get up to after the fact, Changeling is about the urban fantasy trope of what happens when you add fae to modern life,, etc. Mage is about people who can do magic. Sorcerer would also be about people who do magic. It's re-treading the same thematic ground. Since it covers the same themes, it naturally falls in with M20 as far as product placement goes. I don't see how it would be done any other way in non-in-game-context-$$$-sense.<br /> <br /> Here's where I will have my schizoid break:<br /> <br /> Having said that about Sorcerer20's viability as something independent, there is something a little unwieldly about having Mage mages being the exemplars of what the &quot;people who do magic&quot; in the WoD. The interface between themes is clunky, at best. The games are tied to a reality consistent with their theme. Those realities have internal rules and those rules are what shapes how whole situation works. Mage is tied to a mutable reality, in fact, inherently, to reality-BREAKING. That makes interactions dicey. In VtR, vampires can't stop being Vampires without some inherently mystical, earth-shattering secret thing with no known rules. It's a case by case thing with all of the cases lining up with &quot;God says that's okay, but it's a one-time thing for this particular situation.&quot; With Mage it becomes more of a question of which of a 1000 way of creatively applying the rules would work or not work. Or setting up something pretty much arbitrary and saying &quot;Your magick doesn't work... because it doesn't. Vampirism is beyond Mages. They cannot create nor destroy it. Just 'cause.&quot; Any explanation boils down pretty much to that, as there are many many other things that Mages patently CAN do (and have done) to the underlying rules of reality that really make turning Jill back into a human look easy-peasy, say, like making the Earth revolve around the Sun instead of vice-versa and getting the vast majority of people to buy into it.<br /> <br /> So, yes, I really do want some kind of Sorcerer rules. I see a real need for them for the games OTHER than Mage. I think it would be easier to have Sorcerer out there to allow for magic workers that might be more thematically appropriate to their settings. And it seems like it would be easier to take those kind of rules and port them over to each of the other scenes with minimal tweaking needed, much as you have described above.<br /> <br /> Another break:<br /> <br /> For Mage, I really have issues with the 1:3 ratio of Sorcerer:Mage that gets thrown out there. Are there a lot of games out there where a four person cabal is 3 Sorcerers to 1 Mage? If not, are all PC cabals a collection of special snow flakes? The rules are very fluid and somewhat opaque about how Mages of different paradigms work together, how easy (or more appropriately &quot;how hard&quot;) is it for Sorcerers with much more rigid paradigms to do so? Why NOT have all magic workers in Mage be Mages? It doesn't seem to be a problem for Mage<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" />A and it wasn't one for Ars Magica. And it seems like the default of the game Mage is that people are playing Mages, not Sorcerers and, , in most games as played and across the material published, there aren't a lot of Sorcerer's being used or presented. Certainly not 3 to 1. I'm not going to go through all the Mage cabals and chantries and adventures and setting books to count it all up, but I'm pretty sure that the numbers would bear me out on this. So, I wouldn't be too unhappy to see Sorcerers as a concept get downplayed down a lot in M20. Leave Mage to Mages. Sorcerers could be more structured to fulfill the same role that Enchanted do in Changeling, Ghouls do in Vampire, etc.<br /> <br /> And, yup, I do see the dichotomies there. I do want something Sorcerer out there for all the games to use where Sorcerer magic looks a helluva like what Thuamaturgy or Necromancy in Vampire, even though I think it won't fly unless it's a M20 book and I don't want those kind of Sorcerers in M20. (If they are there, I definitely don't want them to be the dark matter of the mage universe... out there, making all the difference in some hard to understand way.., but mostly invisible and not having any readily perceptible effect except in a very limited way.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1062,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","user":"Ajax","date":". 01-02-2015, 01:04 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-02T13:39:17-08:00","id":357709,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ajax</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/357320-sorcerer-20th-anniversary-edition?p=357564#post357564\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Having said that about Sorcerer20's viability as something independent, there is something a little unwieldly about having Mage mages being the exemplars of what the &quot;people who do magic&quot; in the WoD. The interface between themes is clunky, at best.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm not really sure how. The point of mages compared to sorcerers has always supposed to be a juxtaposition. Sorcerers are never presented as servitors granted a bit of power (ghouls, familiars) for their service, or side-effect characters that get a bit of extra oomph from being around their supernatural types (kinfolk, kinain, dhampirs, dhampyrs, revenants). They're more appropriately compared to wraiths vs. mediums; a complimentary relationship that strains because each group comes from a different perspective.<br /> <br /> The themes are supposed to have a bit of inherent contradiction as part of what makes the interactions interesting. I don't see how this is &quot;clunky.&quot; The fact that sorcerers and mages work on different principles are part of Mage's exploration of a mutable reality.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> With Mage it becomes more of a question of which of a 1000 way of creatively applying the rules would work or not work. Or setting up something pretty much arbitrary and saying &quot;Your magick doesn't work... because it doesn't. Vampirism is beyond Mages. They cannot create nor destroy it. Just 'cause.&quot; Any explanation boils down pretty much to that, as there are many many other things that Mages patently CAN do (and have done) to the underlying rules of reality that really make turning Jill back into a human look easy-peasy, say, like making the Earth revolve around the Sun instead of vice-versa and getting the vast majority of people to buy into it. </div> </div> </div>The idea that reversing vamprism permanently is beyond most mages isn't really that arbitrary. There's lots of stuff most mages can't hope to do. Something similar from the books: Senex attempted to disprove the common belief that a Caul inverted Avatar can't be fixed. He did prove that it was at least not as certain as most mages thought. It took him ~200 years to do once, and it was never 100% clear of he succeeded or not. Comparing reversing vamprism to getting humans to commonly accept a heliocentric view of our solar system is pretty much apples and oranges. Before that point, there's no evidence the solar system was actually geocentric. Heliocentric and geocentric views are both ancient, and the metaphysics presented in the books seem to indicate that heliocentric was always right on a spiritual level. At best, like many historical things, at best you can say it was a superimposition of multiple states at once until the Order of Reason/Technocracy sought to collapse it into a single model. This was an effort that also took centuries.<br /> <br /> Mages might be theoretically capable of anything, but there have always been serious limitations on some of the higher end stuff. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> For Mage, I really have issues with the 1:3 ratio of Sorcerer:Mage that gets thrown out there. Are there a lot of games out there where a four person cabal is 3 Sorcerers to 1 Mage? If not, are all PC cabals a collection of special snow flakes? The rules are very fluid and somewhat opaque about how Mages of different paradigms work together, how easy (or more appropriately &quot;how hard&quot;) is it for Sorcerers with much more rigid paradigms to do so? Why NOT have all magic workers in Mage be Mages? </div> </div> </div>This seems to massively misinterpret where the 1:3 ratio came from as a design/setting point. It's a plug to deal with the fact that awakened mages are supposed to be fairly rare, but the organizations presented are too large to mesh with numbers presented.<br /> <br /> Saying there's around 3:1 sorcerers to mages is like saying there's around 2:1 ghouls to vampires, or 10:1 kin to Garou. It to help flush out how these organizations can be as big and influential as they are, while the core top tier supernatural members aren't that common.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Sorcerers could be more structured to fulfill the same role that Enchanted do in Changeling, Ghouls do in Vampire, etc. </div> </div> </div>That's pretty much always been their role, save that they are capable of more independence and power than the other &quot;support&quot; types.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-02T16:40:12-08:00","id":357891,"title":"","content":"Adding vampirism to the list of things that are impossible or nigh-impossible is arbitrary as there are obviously things are that nigh impossible that are done with ease in Mage. It's not arbitrary in that it doesn't subordinate the flagship game, VtM, to Mage. That's not to say it wouldn't be 'nigh impossible' in any case, but it wouldn't get the &quot;Nuh-uh! hand's off the core ideas of another game line!&quot; nigh absolutism that it gets.<br /> <br /> None of that makes the 1:3 ration any more rational. Having mages being rarer than makes sense to make the game functional is somewhat silly. Making up for the lack of other magical humans to staff the Union and Tradition offices by creating a class of near-mages to fill out the ranks with some kind of undefined close relationship to magery that is not magery but kinda like it (it may not be ghouls-vampires, but medium-wraith doesn't really encapsulate it either) seems to miss an easy fix. Just have as many mages as you need to. Which is what most people have done in most games I've seen or heard tell of. I just haven't seen a Chantry described with it's 3-5 mages and 9-15 sorcerers. Or a city or other setting. It is just the same as the 1:1,000,000 vamp to human ratio that makes things like having Princes, Primogen, other vampire offices, Clans insofar as they are political entities above the level of immediate families, etc. completely irrelevant except in the largest metropoli. The only game where &quot;the PCs are part of the super-exceptional in-club&quot; to the point it creates a cognitive disconnect with the game setting and meta-plot is Wraith (where your pool starts with everyone who has died and then narrows down based on some set of semi-nebulous criteria, which, however, don't end with there being, say, 250 wraiths in NYC or something like that) or possibly Changeling. (I guess HtR works as the exception that proves the rule.)<br /> <br /> All that being said, since it's just my own opinion and observation, back to Dreamspeaker's initial post:<br /> <br /> Yes, I do see a definitely slot for what you are talking about, largely in the sense you are talking about. Maybe not with regard to Mage b/c of the massive and contradictory overlap in core concepts. But for all the other games.... there needs to be some kind of &quot;mere mortal&quot; magic user out there and mages are not necessarily the best fit. Having something that works for all the different settings without some of the issues of putting Mage-syle magic users in would be a good thing. Having some way to tailor them to the particular mythology of each game seems like a cool idea as well. If you want to use all the different types of power-sources and have a Baskin Robbins style selection of different flavors of sorcerer or just use the flavor relevant to the game line you are playing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-02T22:41:43-08:00","id":358065,"title":"","content":"A little history here: as I alluded to in my first post, there has always been a dichotomy concerning the role of Hedge Magic in the original World of Darkness. When it first appeared (in its admittedly most primitive form) in <b>Hunters Hunted</b>, its role was to empower mortals to be dangerous from the standpoint of vampires. <b>Ascension\u2019s Right Hand</b> presented them as Custos \u2014 non-mages who assist mages. Each of these views got further support later on: the <b>Hunters Hunted</b> perspective was revisited in several of the <i>Year of the Ally</i> supplements (<b>Inquisition</b> reprinted a few Paths with thematically appropriate name changes and introduced a few new Paths; <b>Halls of the Arcanum</b> introduced an Alchemy Path; <b>The Quick and the Dead</b> featured several ghosthunting groups that weilded Hedge Magic), then in the first edition of <b>Sorcerer</b> (which, among other things, collected all of the Paths thus far published into one place; and in the case of the new Paths introduced in <b>Inquisition</b>, renamed them), and most recently in <b>Hunters Hunted 2</b>. The \u201ccustos\u201d perspective got revisited in <b>Sorcerer Revised</b>. <br /> <br /> It\u2019s worth noting that the 1:3 ratio thing is a <b>Mage Revised</b> thing \u2014 or, more specifically, a <b>Sorcerer Revised</b> thing \u2014 and Satyros has said that it will <i>not</i> be the default assumption in M20: the game will proceed on the assumption that most Mage organizations consist primarily of mages. <br /> <br /> I would not object to a <b>M20 Ascension\u2019s Right Hand</b> book that is to the original, first edition supplement as <b>Hunters Hunted 2</b> is to the original <b>Hunters Hunted</b>; that would be the sort of book that Heavy Arms appears to be advocating. But that should <i>not</i> be what a 20th Anniversary edition of <b>Sorcerer</b> should be; <b>Sorcerer 20</b> should take the broader perspective that the 1997 edition of <b>Sorcerer</b> had, of sorcerers as individuals who are <i>not</i> tied to or subservient to any one gameline. As presented in <b>Sorcerer</b>, they might occasionally be allies or enemies of mages; but no more so than they are allies or enemies of vampires, werewolves, wraiths, or changelings.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-02T23:20:23-08:00","id":358071,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ajax</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/357320-sorcerer-20th-anniversary-edition?p=357891#post357891\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Adding vampirism to the list of things that are impossible or nigh-impossible is arbitrary as there are obviously things are that nigh impossible that are done with ease in Mage. It's not arbitrary in that it doesn't subordinate the flagship game, VtM, to Mage. That's not to say it wouldn't be 'nigh impossible' in any case, but it wouldn't get the &quot;Nuh-uh! hand's off the core ideas of another game line!&quot; nigh absolutism that it gets.</div> </div> </div> </div>Messing with Patterns has always been in the &quot;high end if at all&quot; end of the spectrum. That's why the Pattern Bleed rules exist. Altering human level Patterns has always been a minimum of Sphere mastery for anyone but yourself. There's nothing arbitrary about mages not being able to permanently turn other supernaturals into humans. It would, effectively kill them or enslave them since they'd need quintessence to not die from Pattern Bleeding. Just like what would happen if you gave a Sleeper Strength 6; you're really just killing them if you don't end up turning them into a thaumavore instead (and they'd have no inherent way to feed as a thaumavore).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Just have as many mages as you need to. </div> </div> </div>Except that basically cuts out a huge amount of source inspiration material regarding mages/wizards/etc. having servants with some magical abilities but nothing compared to their masters. Having linears fill out the ranks isn't the only solution, and it might not be as &quot;easy&quot; as just having a lot of mages, but it is a more elegant solution. It's also been how the books have been written for a long time. The 1:3 ratio is newer, but the relationship goes back a ways.<br /> <br /> It's like cutting ghouls out of Vampire. You don't need them. You could replace them with high Generation vampires that can operate in the day because the blood is so weak in them, and any mortal servant is just Dominated/Presenced into doing things. But then you don't really have Renfields.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-03T04:01:28-08:00","id":358185,"title":"","content":"The 1:3 ratio is definitely newer; part of the MRev metaplot was that the Avatar Storm was accompanied by a surge in sorcerers. Admittedly, 2e still had acolytes; but IIRC, the bulk of their contribution to magic was that they could serve as assistants to a given mage without counting as witnesses to his magic. Acolytes who had supernatural abilities of their own were a rarity.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-03T09:28:42-08:00","id":358373,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/357320-sorcerer-20th-anniversary-edition?p=358071#post358071\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Messing with Patterns has always been in the &quot;high end if at all&quot; end of the spectrum. That's why the Pattern Bleed rules exist. Altering human level Patterns has always been a minimum of Sphere mastery for anyone but yourself. There's nothing arbitrary about mages not being able to permanently turn other supernaturals into humans. It would, effectively kill them or enslave them since they'd need quintessence to not die from Pattern Bleeding. Just like what would happen if you gave a Sleeper Strength 6; you're really just killing them if you don't end up turning them into a thaumavore instead (and they'd have no inherent way to feed as a thaumavore).<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Except that basically cuts out a huge amount of source inspiration material regarding mages/wizards/etc. having servants with some magical abilities but nothing compared to their masters. Having linears fill out the ranks isn't the only solution, and it might not be as &quot;easy&quot; as just having a lot of mages, but it is a more elegant solution. It's also been how the books have been written for a long time. The 1:3 ratio is newer, but the relationship goes back a ways.<br /> <br /> It's like cutting ghouls out of Vampire. You don't need them. You could replace them with high Generation vampires that can operate in the day because the blood is so weak in them, and any mortal servant is just Dominated/Presenced into doing things. But then you don't really have Renfields.</div> </div> </div> </div>I would continue my argument that those considerations are still very arbitrary and put in place mostly to keep the integrity of the other games. Mage is all about changing all the rules, even the very hard ones. I'm not holding that changing vampires, werewolves, changelings, etc. would be EASY, it would just not be de facto nigh-on-verboten. As I have said, there are plenty of things as epic or more epic that Mages can do with less overall mastery of Spheres. But I won't in this thread as, I think, Dreamweaver was looking for something in a different direction. Do you want to open up a thread here or in Mage and discuss this further, Heavy?<br /> <br /> (Since we haven't done so as of yet...) In response to the last part, as Dataweaver has pointed out, it was a late addition. It was definitely not something in place during the Book of Chantries (before sorcerers were a much of a glimmer in the designer's collective eye) and it wasn't in play in Las Vegas, the only Mage setting book, which was much further along. It doesn't impact the source material or conception of mage's very much at all. Sorcerers as written don't serve the function of guy-and-gal Fridays, lab assistants or staff. In most respects, they are a <i>replacement </i>for mages. Sure, they could be seen extensions of a mage's authority, like ghouls, if and only if they work (or even with) mages, and, as Dreamweaver seems to be aiming for here, Sorcerers don't really <i>have </i>to have much to do with mages at all. In fact, by the virtues of most of the arguments raised, they probably would end up at odds in a &quot;my magic is better than yours&quot; way. &quot;I'm more mystically flexible!&quot; vs. &quot;I don't have to deal with Paradox!&quot; Getting rid of sorcerers (especially since they weren't a part of the original conception) wouldn't have a smidgin of the effect removing ghouls would from Vampire. (Though that might be an interesting game... projecting power during the day would be MUCH more of a concern and that might even be more in line with much of the source material). If you want to have vampires have the kind of easy power and influence over the world they are portrayed as having in the game, ghouls are an efficient way to expedite that. (To that end Renfield could just as easily be a mortal whose mind has broken under extensive use of Dominate. ghouls don't have to have his.... limitations?)<br /> <br /> I'm thinking, and this may be just my reading of the original post, that Dreamspeaker has a two-fold goal in the thread. 1) The viability of Sorcerer20 as a product in its own right (and not as a Mage20 supplement.) 2) What such independent sorcerers might look like, and how they would both stand alone and/or be specifically tailored/integrated into the other game lines.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-03T11:23:42-08:00","id":358435,"title":"","content":"I used the 1997 Sorcerer a lot in non-Mage games. In a lot of chronicles - whether Vampire, Werewolf, Wraith, or Changeling - the ST has a need for a magical NPC of some type. Maybe it's simply a medium/spiritualist who can contact spirits. Maybe it's someone with knowledge of the occult who assists some monster hunters. Maybe it's someone that a PC with ties to the occult community interacts with. Someone with their own agenda, or who will be an Ally or Antagonist. Someone you can cater to your needs in your chronicle, without the baggage of the Ascension War and Paradox. There is no reason it can't be used in Mage as well, but I actually think it's the game line with least need of it.<br /> <br /> What I would want in a Sorcerer 20 book would be a universal supplement that could be used in any game line. Ideally it would bring in the Numina mentioned in other game books, and bring in all the different kinds of special people. It would be a combination of Sorcerer, Wraith's Mediums and Quick &amp; the Dead, Halls of the Arcanum, some of the Infernalist stuff listed in lots of different game books (i'd also include some old Hunter's stuff, but that's in HH2). Those books all have lots of good information, but also a ton of wasted space and fluff that could easily be condensed.<br /> <br /> To a certain extent, it would be a big book of how to make NPCs, but there would be enough information there for people to run an occult or occult investigator chronicle with PCs. Definitely on the low end of power compared to PCs of the official gamelines, but could be a lot of fun.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-03T21:41:59-08:00","id":358814,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ajax</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/357320-sorcerer-20th-anniversary-edition?p=358373#post358373\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Sorcerers as written don't serve the function of guy-and-gal Fridays, lab assistants or staff. In most respects, they are a <i>replacement </i>for mages. Sure, they could be seen extensions of a mage's authority, like ghouls, if and only if they work (or even with) mages, and, as Dreamweaver seems to be aiming for here, Sorcerers don't really <i>have</i> to have much to do with mages at all. In fact, by the virtues of most of the arguments raised, they probably would end up at odds in a \u201cmy magic is better than yours\u201d way. \u201cI'm more mystically flexible!\u201d vs. \u201cI don't have to deal with Paradox!\u201d Getting rid of sorcerers (especially since they weren't a part of the original conception) wouldn't have a smidgin of the effect removing ghouls would from Vampire.</div> </div> </div> </div>And as I said in my last post, a distinction should be made between \u201csorcerer\u201d (someone who practices Hedge Magic) and \u201cacolyte\u201d or \u201ccustos\u201d (someone who supports mages). While there are some people who are both, the default assumption should be that they\u2019re the exception rather than the rule: most acolytes aren\u2019t sorcerers, and most sorcerers aren\u2019t acolytes. <br /> <br /> Unless you\u2019re operating in the Revised era, of course. <i>Then</i> you can go with the notion that the Avatar Storm caused most acolytes to become sorcerers and started an active recruitment drive that brought most unaffiliated sorcerers into Awakened society. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ajax</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/357320-sorcerer-20th-anniversary-edition?p=358373#post358373\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm thinking, and this may be just my reading of the original post, that Dreamspeaker has a two-fold goal in the thread. 1) The viability of Sorcerer20 as a product in its own right (and not as a Mage20 supplement.) 2) What such independent sorcerers might look like, and how they would both stand alone and/or be specifically tailored/integrated into the other game lines.</div> </div> </div> </div>You got it exactly right. <br /> <br /> On the latter point, and going back to something Heavy Arms said earlier: <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/357320-sorcerer-20th-anniversary-edition?p=357357#post357357\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Trying to have a whole set of Sorcery rules tweaks for different game line metaphysics is also just flat out unnecessary. There don't need to be Gnosis based Numina, or Glamour powered Psychics. How are these any better than, \u201cSorcerers are proto-mages\u201d instead of them being their own thing?</div> </div> </div> </div>First, the \u201cGlamour-powered psychics\u201d thing is a total strawman: I specifically argued <i>against</i> putting Psychic Phenomena in a S20 book (IMHO, psychics and mediums deserve a book of their own, and are done a disservice being squeezed into the same book as sorcerers); and even if they <i>were</i> to be included, I wouldn\u2019t expect Hedge Magic tweaks to be applicable to Psychic Phenomena. As for how tweaking Sorcery to be Gnosis-based is better than \u201csorcerers as proto-mages\u201d, the answer is simple: the former would be an <i>option</i> to be applied by Storytellers who want sorcery to be attributable to <b>Werewolf</b>\u2019s animistic metaphysics; \u201csorcerers are proto-mages\u201d, at least the way you describe it, is a <i>mandate</i>. And note that in my original proposal, I wasn\u2019t arguing that \u201csorcerers are proto-mages\u201d should go away entirely; only that that be presented as <i>another</i> optional set of tweaks to Hedge Magic for Storytellers who want to present sorcerers in that light rather than being the default assumption built into the baseline rules. <br /> <br /> What I\u2019d want to see would come in two parts: a baseline set of Hedge Magic rules and Path write-ups, designed to be as straightforward as possible (probably just like the Hedge Magic material that appears in <b>Hunters Hunted II</b>, but with more Paths), followed by a set of rules options for tuning Sorcery to your own tastes. <i>That\u2019s</i> where the rules changes found in <b>Sorcerer Revised</b> would be found (under the \u201cMage tweaks\u201d section), along with equivalent rules tweaks for other gamelines. Also, \u201cbase it on Glamour\u201d and \u201cbase it on Gnosis\u201d are just two of an assortment of ideas I used to illustrate the possibilities for tweaks \u2014 and they were arguably the two weakest ones. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/357320-sorcerer-20th-anniversary-edition?p=358435#post358435\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I used the 1997 Sorcerer a lot in non-Mage games. In a lot of chronicles - whether Vampire, Werewolf, Wraith, or Changeling - the ST has a need for a magical NPC of some type. Maybe it's simply a medium/spiritualist who can contact spirits. Maybe it's someone with knowledge of the occult who assists some monster hunters. Maybe it's someone that a PC with ties to the occult community interacts with. Someone with their own agenda, or who will be an Ally or Antagonist. Someone you can cater to your needs in your chronicle, without the baggage of the Ascension War and Paradox. There is no reason it can't be used in Mage as well, but I actually think it's the game line with least need of it.</div> </div> </div> </div>This. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/357320-sorcerer-20th-anniversary-edition?p=358435#post358435\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What I would want in a Sorcerer 20 book would be a universal supplement that could be used in any game line. Ideally it would bring in the Numina mentioned in other game books, and bring in all the different kinds of special people. It would be a combination of Sorcerer, Wraith's Mediums and Quick &amp; the Dead, Halls of the Arcanum, some of the Infernalist stuff listed in lots of different game books (i'd also include some old Hunter's stuff, but that's in HH2). Those books all have lots of good information, but also a ton of wasted space and fluff that could easily be condensed.</div> </div> </div> </div>If S20 were to be a Book of Numina in all but name, I\u2019d want it to have a full <i>three</i> sections: one for Sorcery, one for Psychic Phenomena, and one for Faith. I\u2019d want the Psychic Phenomena section to include an option for Merit-based powers along the lines of the Merit-based Mediums featured in <b>Mediums: Speakers with the Dead</b>; and I\u2019d want the Faith chapter to combine material from <b>Inquisition</b> and the <b>Celestial Chorus Tradition Book, Revised</b>, with a brief shout-out in the customization section to <b>Hunter: the Reckoning</b>\u2019s system of Conviction, Creeds, Virtues, and Edges as something that could be adapted into a different take on the notion of supernatural faith-based powers. The only things left out would be the Kinain powers found in <b>the Enchanted</b>, and <i>maybe</i> the deal-with-the-devil powers (I forget what they\u2019re called) that have appeared in various gamelines \u2014 and even <i>that</i> might have a place in such a book (especially if it gets broadened from \u201cdemon-granted powers\u201d to \u201cpowers gained by negotiating with dark forces\u201d). <br /> <br /> But as I said before, I\u2019d rather that psychics and mediums would get their own book instead of being tacked onto a book about sorcerers. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/357320-sorcerer-20th-anniversary-edition?p=358435#post358435\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">To a certain extent, it would be a big book of how to make NPCs, but there would be enough information there for people to run an occult or occult investigator chronicle with PCs. Definitely on the low end of power compared to PCs of the official gamelines, but could be a lot of fun.</div> </div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":421,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","user":"Dataweaver","date":". 01-04-2015, 12:46 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-04T02:58:52-08:00","id":358962,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/357320-sorcerer-20th-anniversary-edition?p=358814#post358814\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Unless you\u2019re operating in the Revised era, of course. <i>Then</i> you can go with the notion that the Avatar Storm caused most acolytes to become sorcerers and started an active recruitment drive that brought most unaffiliated sorcerers into Awakened society.</div> </div> </div> </div>You could, if you wanted, but the books never supported this. They supported the Traditions making a bigger push to grant sorcerers more status and recruit more of them into the fold (they also went after Crafts in Revised the same way). Revised never had sorcerers replace acolytes.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You got it exactly right. </div> </div> </div>Though, on that note, it seems to be that very few people think Sorcerer stands on its own successfully. Numina always come up in support supplements to bigger lines except for WOD: Sorcerer, and WOD: Sorcerer didn't convince WW to keep it a separate book.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> First, the \u201cGlamour-powered psychics\u201d thing is a total strawman: </div> </div> </div>It's not a strawman. It's just a point you want to ignore because it wouldn't be part of your ideal Sorcerer book. But we're not just talking about your ideal book, but the concept of the book in general. Sorcerer, especially as a stand alone project, would need maximum diversity of Numina in it to be successful. Cutting out psychics decreases how much of a toolkit the book can be, which runs counter to your stated desires for the book. It's saying that &quot;power X is metaphysically inherently not compatible with power Y,&quot; rather than having them as possibly different types of things, or possibly just different expressions of tapping into the same core human magic.<br /> <br /> The point of both of those is not to get into the argument about psychics in the book (though I still haven't seen a compelling argument against them), but that &quot;customizing&quot; Numina users for each game line is unnecessary. The point is that it is a human base supernatural ability in the first place. All that each supernatural group needs is a view point from their metaphysics on how exactly that works (which most of them already have in the books anyway).<br /> <br /> The idea that for some odd reason more detailed ritual casting rules is &quot;Mage&quot; specific doesn't communicate anything meaningful to me on this point.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> As for how tweaking Sorcery to be Gnosis-based is better than \u201csorcerers as proto-mages\u201d, the answer is simple: the former would be an <i>option</i> to be applied by Storytellers who want sorcery to be attributable to <b>Werewolf</b>\u2019s animistic metaphysics; \u201csorcerers are proto-mages\u201d, at least the way you describe it, is a <i>mandate</i>. </div> </div> </div>See this is a strawman.<br /> <br /> I never stated &quot;proto-mages&quot; as a mandate. Here's what I said:<br /> <br /> &quot;There's also no need to have explanations for Numina for all the game lines. The idea that hedge mages might be using their powers as focused through their Avatars is not really presented as the absolute truth of how Numina exist (and there's a reason why there's a Merit for proto-mages rather than it being the base-line assumption).&quot;<br /> <br /> If that's me stating a mandate, I don't know what to tell you besides chill out about the fact that people don't all hop on board with your ideas about how the cWOD should handle this stuff. The idea that the books present this any stronger is false. They don't. Sorcerers have always been a question in-character as to if they're proto-mages or something else, and the rules are not built on the assumption that they are.<br /> <br /> So, try again. &quot;Gnosis sorcerers&quot; as an option doesn't stand out against &quot;proto-mage&quot; as an option. It also contradicts loads of WtA material. It's not just &quot;tweaking&quot; things to fit WtA metaphysics (WtA metaphysics already had Numina in them as &quot;weird shit humans do, but it doesn't work on the basis of werewolf magic); it's rewriting WtA metaphysics.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> What I\u2019d want to see would come in two parts: a baseline set of Hedge Magic rules and Path write-ups, designed to be as straightforward as possible (probably just like the Hedge Magic material that appears in <b>Hunters Hunted II</b>, but with more Paths), followed by a set of rules options for tuning Sorcery to your own tastes. </div> </div> </div>So... you want Sorcerer Revised but with the expanded ritual rules and some of the optional boxes ported to the back of the book where tweaking things was discussed in more detail.<br /> <br /> All that was already in the book. Even discussions of other game lines. The problem with adding more &quot;setting&quot; tweaks is that Numina were a cWOD-wide thing already. The Sorcerer Revised Path expansions weren't a &quot;making it more Mage&quot; tweak. It was a &quot;making it more robust as a stand alone thing to play&quot; tweak. &quot;Proto-mages&quot; makes sense as presented options because it makes sense in Mage's metaphysics. &quot;Gnosis powered sorcerers&quot; makes zero sense in WtA metaphysics. Gnosis doesn't do that. Studying spirits isn't going to get you Gnosis like it could cause an epiphany leading to an Awakening. You say it's one of the weakest... but besides Mage... where is a strong one? Vampire based &quot;hedge&quot; magic is Ghouls with Blood Sorcery, human Numina are supposed to constrast that. Wraith and Changeling offer flavor with no needed mechanical tweaks at best.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> This. </div> </div> </div>Oddly, I've seen plenty of people use Sorcerer Revised the same way. Being a purple book doesn't stop you from making a Kinfolk sorcerer with it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If S20 were to be a Book of Numina in all but name, I\u2019d want it to have a full <i>three</i> sections: one for Sorcery, one for Psychic Phenomena, and one for Faith. </div> </div> </div>Why Faith? Faith doesn't really have the material to compete with the other two, and would have to be a massive reprint of material from Hunters Hunted II. We're talking... 6 pages of material since True Faith is the only single path category of the three types. There's no way to give it equal coverage. It is just going to seem like filler or page count that could have gone to something more useful.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> ...with a brief shout-out in the customization section to <b>Hunter: the Reckoning</b>\u2019s system of Conviction, Creeds, Virtues, and Edges as something that could be adapted into a different take on the notion of supernatural faith-based powers. </div> </div> </div>Ok... so 7 pages now? And if there's going to be a shout out like this Dark Ages Inquisition (since it models of this already) seems more useful.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The only things left out would be the Kinain powers found in <b>the Enchanted</b>, and <i>maybe</i> the deal-with-the-devil powers (I forget what they\u2019re called) that have appeared in various gamelines \u2014 and even <i>that</i> might have a place in such a book (especially if it gets broadened from \u201cdemon-granted powers\u201d to \u201cpowers gained by negotiating with dark forces\u201d). </div> </div> </div>These aren't Numina... why include them?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But as I said before, I\u2019d rather that psychics and mediums would get their own book instead of being tacked onto a book about sorcerers. </div> </div> </div>But since a book solely about sorcerers probably isn't viable, a book about Numina as a whole is. Though I'm not sure why you keep tagging &quot;support&quot; types that don't use Numina in this (kinain and mediums).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-05T11:51:40-08:00","id":360390,"title":"","content":"I think you had me until the last paragraph, on multiple accounts. It's not bad, but I think it should be an optional ruling if you're adamant about it.<br /> <br /> Also, as someone who rather likes Psychic phenomena, I would...admittedly like to have those included, especially for those who might want to use the &quot;Psychic Phenomena as Paths&quot; rulings. Still, I comprehend your sentiments regarding space to an appreciable degree; I just doubt that, assuming they would deign to release a Sorcerer 20 book in the first place, they might have the time and funds to pump out a Psychic Companion book. (...Though, if they <i>did</i>...a book referencing nothing but, and maybe expanding upon, psychics a bit would be very welcomed on my certain anime nerd part.)<br /> <br /> I'm also of the opinion that I would like a Sorcerer core book, and to let Sorcery be its own thing, but I also agree with Heavy Arms. It isn't likely that we would get such a thing without it being a tie-in to Mage. Just to get one in the first place would be incredible, and if it had to be a companion to Mage to make it more feasible that it would ever come to be, I'd be more than just a little happy to settle for that.<br /> <br /> Regardless, thank you for provoking this topic. It actually made me join the forums, just to add my .5 cents.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#b22222\"><b>I. Found. You.</b></span>","edited":{"userid":4676,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4676-silentsloth","user":"SilentSloth","date":". 01-05-2015, 08:48 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4676-silentsloth","name":"SilentSloth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4676_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"16"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-05T14:43:38-08:00","id":360624,"title":"","content":"As I have mentioned a dozen times about Sorcerer Revised in the threads that have brought it up, Sorcerer Revised was terribly edited. It was basically copied and pasted from 2e Sorcerer withou actually rewriting the rules. There are Abilities used to for the powers that aren't normally on the Mage Revised sheet but are on the Mage 2e sheet. Regardless, Sorcerer needs to be cleaned up, and rules need to be established on how the magic works. Optional rules are great, but there are some terrifyingly annoying problems in the Revised copy. Such as Mana not being Quintessence, but according to Mummy, Mummy is Sekhem, and Sekhem is Quintessence. Stuff like that needs to be worked out.<br /> <br /> What I would enjoy to see is Mummy and Sorcerer being mixed to work together, as Mummies and Sorcerers are one in the same, just Mummies have some more going for the (like being able to fuel spells with Sekhem/Lifeforce/Mana). Sorcerer Revised also mentioned converting Mummy spells into a path. Sorcerer also had some problems of Alchemy and Enchanting being almost impossible to use along with the other all ritual paths. Removing all Ritual and all Spell paths and just mixing it so that Rituals are akin to Rotes (read: well practiced and known spells that may or may not take longer and have more powerful effects than On the Fly spells) would make more sense. I very much like how Revised did the Aspect system, I thought it was rather clever and good. The Mana system should really be sorted out, I'm all for a Sorcerer being able to use Willpower and Mana so they're not limited to 10 spells per day, and can cast their more potent spells more frequently. They're wizards still, but they're not as quick or as powerful as Mages, but should still be able to throw a fireballs and such.<br /> <br /> Another thing that should be cleaned up is using Sorcery as a supernatural. True Magick kinda shits on Sorcery, but why would Order of Hermes mages even bother to learn it then if True Magick is a thing? Besides the failed student approach, I think a good answer is Paradox free magic and if you really want to push it, magic that can be used to make True Magick coincidental. But that's just my 2 cents. Vampires being able to use Thaumaturgy makes sense, but Sorcery, that's a bit different. I'd love to have a system which converts Thaumaturgy to be more Sorcery like than the straight linear increase and rituals that Thaumaturgy has.<br /> <br /> I've played more than one Sorcerer and I really enjoyed it, but damn does Sorcerer Revised have some problems, which could have been easily fixed with some editing. All in all, Sorcerers are really fun to play and I'd love to see a 20th Edition of the book, but please call the editors if it gets made.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1VN49Y7tH2ZV0l7Hx65RWX1ez-Q9Lst-8ZTyTJeCTpxM/\" target=\"_blank\">Sorcerer 20th</a> homebrew and my update to <a href=\"http://immortalthegathering.blogspot.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Highlander: the Gathering</a> for 20th Anniversary edition.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2414-theoutlander523","name":"theoutlander523","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2414_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"238"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-05T16:47:08-08:00","id":360747,"title":"","content":"Amen to Sorcerer Revised having a better editor. The &quot;still using 2E Abilities in an edition that no longer uses them&quot; part was bad enough, but then you had misprints on some of the new stuff (Shapeshifting's Aspects, I'm looking at you) that made for a &quot;wait, what?&quot; moment the first time you read through it.<br /> <br /> The way I interpreted it, that was more or less how rituals worked: that they were essentially longer-cast-time, practiced rotes, which could be one step stronger than spells of equivalent Path level. Or when creating them, they let you cast an effect with a difficulty comparable to one Path/Aspect level lower, with the rationale being that you'd practiced it and turned it into a rote and thereby made it easier to perform. And maybe, if you and your Storyteller can agree on the effect (or you roll six or more successes to finalize the ritual), maybe you could make a ritual that just pertains to a Path, yet doesn't quite use all of its Aspects (i.e. Fire's Weal under Hellfire).<br /> ...I was wrong, wasn't I...?<br /> <br /> Being able to cast spells with Mana in addition to Willpower would be nice, but if the Storyteller wants to keep that restriction in place for thematic reasons, I wouldn't complain, personally. It just means you'd have to either be discerning, or simply advance your Path understanding to a level you can cast lower effects sans cost, for the paths that call for one. Yes, it's more difficult, but it encourages strategy over devil-may-care spellslinging.<br /> Speaking of Mana-for-fufilling-spell-cost, if I were going to use a system like that, I'd state that using some method of replenishing it would take ten minutes per point restored, just to keep Sorcerers from abusing it.<br /> <br /> I believe what they were thinking was that the Order of Hermes only bothers to teach it to new students because they want their True Magick practitioners to have a Sorcerer sort of mindset and approach to True Magick. Yes, it's a bit ironic that they sneer down their nose at &quot;mere&quot; hedge magicians, yet expound their practices and methodology, but that's just the sort of complicated, conflicting and sometimes convoluted politics the Order is trapped up in.<br /> Edit: And if I might hazard a guess, it might be advantageous for them to have acolytes to aid in some rituals, since it would likely be easier to owe them favors than it would to a &quot;proper&quot; member of the Order of Hermes. But, I'm probably mistaken.<br /> <br /> I've actually been trying to edit Revised a little myself, but largely to use with a whole other game setting. It's a daunting task so far... I don't envy whoever would theoretically do it officially.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#b22222\"><b>I. Found. You.</b></span>","edited":{"userid":4676,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4676-silentsloth","user":"SilentSloth","date":". 01-05-2015, 09:08 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4676-silentsloth","name":"SilentSloth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4676_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"16"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-05T20:09:04-08:00","id":360876,"title":"","content":"As I\u2019ve stated before, I\u2019m not opposed to there being a M20 supplement that deals with Numina in general (as opposed to Hedge Magic in particular) from a Mage perspective. But I don\u2019t think that such a book should be called \u201cSorcerer\u201d; I think that label should be reserved for \u201cHedge Magic from a gameline-neutral perspective\u201d. The sort of book that Heavy Arms is advocating is more of a \u201cM20 Ascension\u2019s Right Hand\u201d book, which I also think would be cool; but it oughtn\u2019t be \u201cSorcerer\u201d. <br /> <br /> And yes, I think that <b>Sorcerer Revised</b> shouldn\u2019t have been called \u201cSorcerer\u201d, for this very reason. I consider the book to have been a misstep brought about by White Wolf\u2019s marketing department insisting on its publication as a sales ploy and resulting in a book that wasn\u2019t <i>nearly</i> as thought through as it needed to be (an opinion shared by Satyros Phil Brucato, FWIW). It has a few good ideas in it; but by and large, I think S20 would be better off looking to the 1997 Sorcerer book as its main source of inspiration, with the gems from SRev being dug out of it, polished up, and added to the mix. <br /> <br /> If you want to post \u201cunofficial errata\u201d of <b>Sorcerer Revised</b> here, please go ahead; it definitely fits under the rubric of \u201cwhat ought to be changed in a hypothetical Sorcerer 20\u201d, and was in fact one of the things I was hoping this topic could cover. Likewise, if you\u2019d like to list what you consider to be <b>Sorcerer Revised</b>\u2019s \u201cgems\u201d, feel free. Granted, this thread isn\u2019t <i>primarily</i> about what SRev got right and what it got wrong; but such things would be instructive as to what a hypothetical S20 ought to be like.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-05T20:43:38-08:00","id":360901,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>theoutlander523</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/357320-sorcerer-20th-anniversary-edition?p=360624#post360624\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What I would enjoy to see is Mummy and Sorcerer being mixed to work together, as Mummies and Sorcerers are one in the same, just Mummies have some more going for the (like being able to fuel spells with Sekhem/Lifeforce/Mana). Sorcerer Revised also mentioned converting Mummy spells into a path. Sorcerer also had some problems of Alchemy and Enchanting being almost impossible to use along with the other all ritual paths. Removing all Ritual and all Spell paths and just mixing it so that Rituals are akin to Rotes (read: well practiced and known spells that may or may not take longer and have more powerful effects than On the Fly spells) would make more sense. I very much like how Revised did the Aspect system, I thought it was rather clever and good. The Mana system should really be sorted out, I'm all for a Sorcerer being able to use Willpower and Mana so they're not limited to 10 spells per day, and can cast their more potent spells more frequently. They're wizards still, but they're not as quick or as powerful as Mages, but should still be able to throw a fireballs and such.<br /> <br /> Another thing that should be cleaned up is using Sorcery as a supernatural. True Magick kinda shits on Sorcery, but why would Order of Hermes mages even bother to learn it then if True Magick is a thing? Besides the failed student approach, I think a good answer is Paradox free magic and if you really want to push it, magic that can be used to make True Magick coincidental. But that's just my 2 cents. Vampires being able to use Thaumaturgy makes sense, but Sorcery, that's a bit different. I'd love to have a system which converts Thaumaturgy to be more Sorcery like than the straight linear increase and rituals that Thaumaturgy has.</div> </div> </div> </div>A few thoughts here: <br /> <br /> \u2022 While I agree in principle with your thoughts re: <b>Mummy</b>, I\u2019d be leery of S20 flat-out converting <b>Mummy</b>\u2019s Hekau into Hedge Magic Paths, any more than I\u2019d want to see <b>Vampire</b>\u2019s Thaumaturgy converted into Hedge Magic Paths. Some tweaks for making Sorcery fit into <b>Mummy</b>\u2019s metaphysics would be in order, though. <br /> <br /> \u2022 OTOH, I <i>would</i> appreciate a \u201chow to create new Paths\u201d section, as Hedge Magic\u2019s existing Paths are not and should not be a definitive set of what Hedge Magic covers; and I could see that section including an example of using a Hekau as inspiration for crafting an \u201cEgyptian sorcery\u201d-themed Path. <br /> <br /> \u2022 Mana is definitely something I\u2019d like to see S20 present as a \u201cpower-up\u201d for those who want sorcerers to be able to keep stride with protagonists of the major gamelines; but I wouldn\u2019t want it as part of the baseline: <i>that</i> should be kept as compatible as possible with <b>Hunters Hunted 2</b>.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-05T22:47:59-08:00","id":361013,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/357320-sorcerer-20th-anniversary-edition?p=358814#post358814\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">and <i>maybe</i> the deal-with-the-devil powers (I forget what they\u2019re called) that have appeared in various gamelines \u2014 and even <i>that</i> might have a place in such a book (especially if it gets broadened from \u201cdemon-granted powers\u201d to \u201cpowers gained by negotiating with dark forces\u201d).</div> </div> </div> </div>I believe you are thinking of &quot;demonic investments&quot;.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/357320-sorcerer-20th-anniversary-edition?p=358814#post358814\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But as I said before, I\u2019d rather that psychics and mediums would get their own book instead of being tacked onto a book about sorcerers.</div> </div> </div> </div>I could see psychic/medium material being its own book, but it better be damn good. Philosophically, I can see the distinction between sorcerers and mediums, and how they are different enough to be developed separately. In actuality, for my needs as an ST, I'd want one book for both.<br /> <br /> My main issue is that too many WoD supplements had far too little meat and a lot of useless fat. I'm not talking about good flavor text. I am talking meaningless text that fails to communicate material efficiently or in general fails to add value. Every Werewolf Tribebook, for example, wasted about seven pages on repeating the Litany and how each tribe saw it (which was 95% identical to the material in every other tribe book). That is space that could have been used to communicate something new. Same could be said for any other game line. A 90 page book maybe had a good 15-20 pages of value.<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-05T23:01:03-08:00","id":361026,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/357320-sorcerer-20th-anniversary-edition?p=361013#post361013\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I could see psychic/medium material being its own book, but it better be damn good. Philosophically, I can see the distinction between sorcerers and mediums, and how they are different enough to be developed separately. In actuality, for my needs as an ST, I'd want one book for both.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes. I feel like it isn't likely to happen, but if it did, I would be extremely happy.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#b22222\"><b>I. Found. You.</b></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4676-silentsloth","name":"SilentSloth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4676_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"16"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}